      "updated": 1784655865934,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Component architecture: favor composition over inheritance — build small, focused components and assemble them via props/children rather than base-class hierarchies.\n- Container/Presentational split: separate data-fetching and state logic (containers/hooks) from pure rendering (presenters); presenters take only explicit props and have no side effects.\n- Extract a component when: markup repeats, a chunk grows a distinct responsibility, or you need to test/reuse it in isolation — not preemptively \"for later.\"\n- State management: keep state as local as possible; lift it only when sibling components actually need to share it.\n  - Reach for Context for a few components needing read-mostly shared state (theme, auth) — Context is not a performance-free global store.\n  - Reach for Redux/Zustand when you have complex cross-cutting state, need time-travel/devtools, or many unrelated components mutate the same data.\n  - Prefer composition (pass components as children/slots) over a global store as the first fix for prop drilling.\n- Rendering strategy tradeoffs: CSR (best for highly interactive apps, worse SEO/first paint), SSR (fresh data per request, higher server cost), SSG (fastest, stale until rebuild), ISR (SSG + background revalidation — best of both for content that changes occasionally).\n- Next.js App Router: Server Components by default (data fetching, secrets, zero client JS shipped); add 'use client' only for interactivity/hooks/browser APIs, and push client boundaries as deep (leaf) as possible.\n- Streaming: wrap slow data-fetching components in Suspense boundaries so the page shell renders immediately instead of blocking on the slowest request.\n- Performance — code splitting: use dynamic import()/next/dynamic for below-the-fold or rarely-used heavy components (charts, rich-text editors, modals).\n- Memoization pitfalls: useMemo/useCallback/React.memo add real overhead and complexity — apply them only after profiling shows an actual re-render cost, not defensively everywhere.\n- Core Web Vitals: optimize LCP (priority-load the hero image/text), CLS (reserve space for images/ads/fonts to avoid layout shift), and INP (keep the main thread free, break up long tasks).\n- Bundle-size discipline: audit with a bundle analyzer, avoid pulling in heavy dependencies for small utilities, rely on tree-shaking, and prefer native platform APIs where reasonable.\n- Accessibility: reach for semantic HTML first (button, nav, header, main) before ARIA; use ARIA only to fill gaps native elements cannot express.\n- Keyboard navigation and focus management: every interactive element must be reachable and operable via keyboard; manage focus explicitly on route change, modal open/close, and dynamic content updates.\n- Testing: unit test pure logic and hooks; use component tests (React Testing Library) for user-facing behavior and interaction rather than implementation details; reserve e2e (Playwright/Cypress) for a small set of high-value critical happy-path flows.\n- TypeScript discipline: never reach for `any` as an escape hatch — use `unknown` plus narrowing; model variants with discriminated unions instead of optional-flag soup; use generics for reusable components (e.g., a generic `List<T>`) to preserve type safety across call sites.\n- Interview archetypes to be ready to reason through: autocomplete (debounce input, cancel in-flight requests, keyboard nav, ARIA combobox pattern), virtualized list (windowing, fixed vs. variable row height), infinite scroll (IntersectionObserver, cursor-based pagination, avoiding layout jump on load).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Component architecture: favor composition over inheritance — build small, focused components and assemble them via props/children rather than base-class hierarchies.\n- Container/Presentational split: separate data-fetching and state logic (containers/hooks) from pure rendering (presenters); presenters take only explicit props and have no side effects.\n- Extract a component when: markup repeats, a chunk grows a distinct responsibility, or you need to test/reuse it in isolation — not preemptively \"for later.\"\n- State management: keep state as local as possible; lift it only when sibling components actually need to share it.\n  - Reach for Context for a few components needing read-mostly shared state (theme, auth) — Context is not a performance-free global store.\n  - Reach for Redux/Zustand when you have complex cross-cutting state, need time-travel/devtools, or many unrelated components mutate the same data.\n  - Prefer composition (pass components as children/slots) over a global store as the first fix for prop drilling.\n- Rendering strategy tradeoffs: CSR (best for highly interactive apps, worse SEO/first paint), SSR (fresh data per request, higher server cost), SSG (fastest, stale until rebuild), ISR (SSG + background revalidation — best of both for content that changes occasionally).\n- Next.js App Router: Server Components by default (data fetching, secrets, zero client JS shipped); add 'use client' only for interactivity/hooks/browser APIs, and push client boundaries as deep (leaf) as possible.\n- Streaming: wrap slow data-fetching components in Suspense boundaries so the page shell renders immediately instead of blocking on the slowest request.\n- Performance — code splitting: use dynamic import()/next/dynamic for below-the-fold or rarely-used heavy components (charts, rich-text editors, modals).\n- Memoization pitfalls: useMemo/useCallback/React.memo add real overhead and complexity — apply them only after profiling shows an actual re-render cost, not defensively everywhere.\n- Core Web Vitals: optimize LCP (priority-load the hero image/text), CLS (reserve space for images/ads/fonts to avoid layout shift), and INP (keep the main thread free, break up long tasks).\n- Bundle-size discipline: audit with a bundle analyzer, avoid pulling in heavy dependencies for small utilities, rely on tree-shaking, and prefer native platform APIs where reasonable.\n- Accessibility: reach for semantic HTML first (button, nav, header, main) before ARIA; use ARIA only to fill gaps native elements cannot express.\n- Keyboard navigation and focus management: every interactive element must be reachable and operable via keyboard; manage focus explicitly on route change, modal open/close, and dynamic content updates.\n- Testing: unit test pure logic and hooks; use component tests (React Testing Library) for user-facing behavior and interaction rather than implementation details; reserve e2e (Playwright/Cypress) for a small set of high-value critical happy-path flows.\n- TypeScript discipline: never reach for `any` as an escape hatch — use `unknown` plus narrowing; model variants with discriminated unions instead of optional-flag soup; use generics for reusable components (e.g., a generic `List<T>`) to preserve type safety across call sites.\n- Interview archetypes to be ready to reason through: autocomplete (debounce input, cancel in-flight requests, keyboard nav, ARIA combobox pattern), virtualized list (windowing, fixed vs. variable row height), infinite scroll (IntersectionObserver, cursor-based pagination, avoiding layout jump on load).",
